# An effect written inside a `build` body, on any line but the last, used to
# vanish. `build` is a statement with no value — what it built is in scope
# after it — so its body is for construction, and a bare effect line there is
# a description nobody joined. That is exactly what this diagnostic exists to
# say, and until the check was added to the walker that handles `build` and
# block bodies, nothing said it: three `io/write` lines in a build ran the
# last one and lost the other two, in silence, on both engines.
#
# The outer walk had the rule all along. `reject_never_effect` in the parser
# refuses a non-final literal, list, map, lambda or binary operation, and the
# lexer folds consecutive expression lines into one group — so at the top
# level the case cannot arise, which is what the excused-diagnostics list
# concluded. It read one walker of two.
import "std/io"

type node
  next

build
  n = node none
  io/write "a\n"
  io/write "b\n"
  io/write "n={n.next}\n"
